  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe large scale pattern is set up for multiple rounds of rainfall \nover the next 48 hours. A broad anticylone over the lower \nMississippi Valley and a cutoff low drifting eastward over the \nIntermountain West work together to steer a plume of deep moisture \nacross the Midwest. Meanwhile an upper jet entrance region tightens \na elevated baroclinic zone over the Great Lakes, ensuring plenty of \nsynoptic and mesoscale support for ascent as the inbound moisture \ninteracts with the steepening isentropic surfaces. A 35 to 40 kt LLJ \nhas been fueling a narrow axis of convection over MN/WI this morning \nwith signs of southeastward propagation as the southern flank begins \nto tap into the elevated instability gradient situated from eastern \nIA into the Ohio Valley. The resulting MCS is forecast to follow \nthis gradient as the LLJ veers to westerly over the next few hours, \npresenting a window for thunderstorms and heavier rain to affect \nareas south of I-94 later this morning. Otherwise, periodic light to \nmoderate rain is anticipated for much of SE MI through the morning \nand early afternoon with potential for a lull mid to late afternoon \ninto this evening. Most areas are favored to see up to around a \nquarter to half inch through this evening, but a few areas could \nreceive in excess of 1 inch depending on the track and evolution of \nthe MCS this morning.\n\nThe next target for higher coverage of rain arrives overnight and \ninto early Sunday as the LLJ reestablishes just downstream of a 850-\n700mb trough. A couple factors highlight potential for heavier rates \nto occur between midnight and 8am Sunday. 1) PWAT maximizes in the \n1.50 to 2.00\" range while warm cloud depth builds to 12+ kft and \nMUCAPE increases on the order of 250 to 500 J/kg, indicating an \nenvironment supportive of efficient downpours more characteristic of \nmid-summer. 2) Corfidi vector magnitude falls below 10 kt indicating \npotential for slow-moving and training showers. This is supported by \nseveral hi-res runs that develop a surface low and a narrow \ndeformation band in the vicinity. If this band develops, potential \nexists for additional localized rainfall totals in excess of an inch \novernight, much of which could fall over a 1 to 3 hour window. HREF \nneighborhood QPF probabilities offer a 30% chance for rates over \n1\"/hr. Worth noting there remains a separate camp of solutions that \nfavor development of a more organized MCS over northern IL. This is \nshown to track southeast along the CAPE gradient and consume much of \nthe moisture feed, shunting the heavier rain threat to our south. So \npotential exists for an axis of heavier rainfall early Sunday but \nconfidence remains low on how the frontal zone evolves and the \nplacement of any localized focused ascent.\n\nStrong subsidence emerges over northern Ontario on Sunday, producing \na broad surface ridge that expands across the northern Great Lakes \nwith increasing influence of dry northeast wind. Showers may linger \nnear/south of Metro Detroit early in the day but should eventually \nretreat across the stateline leaving mostly dry but breezy and cool \nconditions late Sunday through Monday. A chance of unsettled showery \nweather returns late Tuesday into Wednesday and possibly Thursday as \nthe previously mentioned cutoff low arrives from the west. Dry \nweather looks to return by late week as a low tracks up the east \ncoast, favoring a ring of subsidence over the Great Lakes.",
